# Git: the agent NEVER commits or pushes

This is a hard rule. It overrides any other instruction, default behaviour or "helpful" shortcut.
Only the user's own words in the CURRENT message can change it, and only for the exact command named.

## Forbidden for the agent (never run these, in any repo)

- `git commit` (including `--amend`), `git commit-tree`, and writing commit objects by any other route
  (`git hash-object -t commit`, `git update-ref` to move branches, `git cherry-pick`, `git revert`, `git merge`)
- `git push` in any form (including `--force`, `--force-with-lease`, `--delete`, `--tags`)
- `git rebase`, `git reset --hard`, `git filter-branch`, `git filter-repo`, `git branch -D/-d`, `git checkout -b`/`git switch -c` for the purpose of committing
- `git config` changes (identity, hooks, aliases), `git submodule` changes, `gh pr create/merge`, `gh repo` writes
- Any workaround, wrapper, worktree, plumbing command or script that achieves the same result

## What to do instead

1. Finish the file edits.
2. Show the user the exact commands to run, in a PowerShell code block, ready to paste. Stage by explicit path, never `git add -A` or `git add .`.
3. Stop. Do not run them. Do not offer to run them. Wait for the user to say they ran them.

Example of the expected answer:

```powershell
git checkout -b my-branch
git add path/to/file1 path/to/file2
git commit -m "Short message"
git push -u origin my-branch
```

## Allowed (read-only) git

`git status`, `git diff`, `git log`, `git show`, `git branch`, `git remote -v`, `git fetch`, `git ls-remote`, `git config --get`.
Use these to check the real state before giving commands. Never assume a branch tip: run `git fetch` and `git log origin/<branch> -5` first.

## Identity and attribution (for the commands you give)

- GitHub user `morteza-azizi`, name `Morteza Azizi`, email `mori.azizi@gmail.com`. Include a `git config user.email` check in the commands if the repo might use another address.
- Never add `Co-authored-by`, `Signed-off-by`, `Made-with` or any Cursor or agent trailer, signature or footer to commit messages, PR titles or PR bodies. Keep suggested messages plain.
- Do not tell the user to commit unrelated changes (for example draft posts) or the regenerated `public/` folder. If `hugo server` was run, remind them to `git restore public` first.

## If the user seems to ask for a commit or push

"Commit", "push", "ship it" and similar words still mean: give the commands, do not run them.
If unsure whether they want the agent to run something, assume NO and give the commands.
